{"duration_api_ms":307938,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"cab16172-cf1b-4218-a821-8f49e9f4826d","total_cost_usd":1.4836878000000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":28,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71949,"cache_read_input_tokens":822319,"output_tokens":37176,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":9838},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71949,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1229,"cache_read_input_tokens":82269,"cache_creation_input_tokens":514,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":514},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":28,"outputTokens":37176,"cacheReadInputTokens":822319,"cacheCreationInputTokens":71949,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.4836878000000002,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":9838,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":15,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I wrote the test suite in `tests/test_agent.py`. All 330 tests pass with the command you gave, they take about 0.3 seconds, and I didn't change the library code.\n\n**What the tests cover:**\n- **Date parsing:** every basic and extended format, plus expanded years and signs. It includes week dates near year boundaries, where ISO week 1 starts in the previous year, ordinal dates in leap years, and the default month/day settings. It also checks that bad or out-of-range input raises an error and that the parsing patterns are cached.\n- **Time parsing:** decimal fractions of hours, minutes and seconds with either `.` or `,`. Microseconds are truncated rather than rounded, and times starting with `0` are covered. Time zones are tested as `Z`, `±hh`, `±hhmm` and `±hh:mm`, including negative offsets with minutes.\n- **Formatting:** every predefined date, time, datetime and duration format, the `yeardigits` option, `%C`, `%j`, `%w`, `%%`, and unknown codes that pass through unchanged. It also covers the edge cases of the time-zone formatter: UTC printed as `Z`, a zero offset with daylight saving, a missing offset, offsets over 99 hours, and an invalid format string.\n- **Durations:** the `PnYnMnDTnHnMnS` form, the alternative `PYYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss` form, weeks, fractions, signs, returning a `timedelta` versus a `Duration`, and round trips back to text.\n- **The `Duration` class:** arithmetic with dates, including month-end clamping (Jan 31 + 1 month gives Feb 29 or Feb 28), adding and subtracting with carries across years, multiplication, negation, `==` and `!=` in both directions, hashing, `str` and `repr`, pickling and `totimedelta`.\n- **Time-zone classes:** `UTC` and `FixedOffset`. For the local time zone, the tests start a separate Python process with `TZ=America/New_York` and `TZ=UTC`, so the daylight-saving logic is checked against known values. The New York test skips itself if the machine has no time-zone database.\n\n**My own mutation check:** I ran a quick mutation script against a scratch copy of the repo. It made 621 single-line mutants (flipped comparisons, changed constants, deleted statements), and the tests catch all but 35. I went through those 35 and believe all but one change nothing you can observe:\n- code that can never run, such as the fallback `return match.group(0)` lines in `isostrf.py` and the error branch in `parse_tzinfo`;\n- defaults that every caller overrides;\n- `>` versus `>=` in month-end clamping, which gives the same result either way;\n- rounding constants with the same precision.\n\nThe one real survivor is the version string, which I chose not to pin because that test would break on every release.\n\nTwo things I found along the way:\n- **The week-date formatter uses the calendar year, not the ISO week year.** For example, 2010-01-03 is formatted as `2010-W53-7` when ISO 8601 says `2009-W53-7`. The test expects the library's current output, with a comment noting this. If you treat it as a bug, that test will need updating when it's fixed.\n- **My first mutation run missed one survivor.** The New York test's skip check depended on the code being tested, so that mutant made the test skip itself instead of fail. The skip now reads the system's time-zone name directly, so it no longer depends on the library.\n\nThe test runs also created an untracked `isodate/__pycache__/` folder, which you can delete.","ttft_ms":2885,"type":"result","duration_ms":1089016,"uuid":"d20b5a3d-9773-4837-8c32-c6552f193cba","ttft_stream_ms":1647,"time_to_request_ms":211,"first_content_frame_ms":1647,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

